12. /etc/sysctl.conf is a configuration file to set kernel parameters permanently
It is used to tune Linux kernel behavior without reboot (after reload)
These parameters are related to:
-- Networking
-- Memory management
-- Security
-- File system limits

How it works:
Values written in this file are applied using:
sysctl -p (reloads config)
Settings persist across reboots (if system reads this file during boot)

View all kernel parameters
sysctl -a

View specific parameter
sysctl net.ipv4.ip_forward

Set parameter temporarily (runtime only)
sysctl -w net.ipv4.ip_forward=1

Apply settings from config file
sysctl -p




